nce, steps 14-15). The sole candidate relay 1583 is the graph's second-busiest hub (773 events, 540 partners, 2712 triangles; steps 08-10), so a static 303-1583-1293 path is exactly the kind of hub-mediated coincidence that arises without any influence flow. Crucially, every available tool aggregates over the whole horizon and returns no timestamps, so I cannot verify that the 303->1583 event precedes the 1583->1293 event, that both fall inside [28296, 66695], or that their gap is <= 600 time units - a very tight constraint, roughly 1.6% of the 38399-unit window (step_16). Without any temporal ordering evidence the cascade can be neither confirmed nor refuted.", "confidence": 0.5}, "raw_final_response": "```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_44f03991\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [303, 1293, 1583, 726],\n  \"window\": [28296, 66695],\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"Structurally there is exactly one directed 2-hop route from 303 to 1293: 303->1583 (1 event, step_06) and 1583->1293 (1 event, step_07). Both 303 (11 events, 11 partners) and 1293 (13 events, 13 partners) are low-activity nodes (step_02), and 1293's 1-hop neighbourhood (step_12) shows no other in-neighbour reachable from 303's out-neighbours (641 is unconnected to 1293, step_13; the 303-726 edge is out-going but the 726/1293 edge runs 1293->726, so it cannot carry influence, steps 14-15). The sole candidate relay 1583 is the graph's second-busiest hub (773 events, 540 partners, 2712 triangles; steps 08-10), so a static 303-1583-1293 path is exactly the kind of hub-mediated coincidence that arises without any influence flow. Crucially, every available tool aggregates over the whole horizon and returns no timestamps, so I cannot verify that the 303->1583 event precedes the 1583->1293 event, that both fall inside [28296, 66695], or that their gap is <= 600 time units - a very tight constraint, roughly 1.6% of the 38399-unit window (step_16). Without any temporal ordering evidence the cascade can be neither confirmed nor refuted.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 43394, "total_output_tokens": 5335, "total_tokens": 48729, "total_latency_ms": 79596, "llm_calls": 9, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}